.he "sort (1)                     9/30/26                     sort (1)"
.fo "                               -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd
NAME
.in +5
.br
.bd
sort
- sort and merge text files
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SYNTAX
.in +5
.br
.bd
sort [ -bdfimnru ] [ +pos [ -pos ] ] [ -o output ] [ -T dir ] [ file ... ]
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
DESCRIPTION
.in +5
.br
All of the named input files are sorted together and the result is
written to the standard output, unless an output file is named with
the -o option.
.sp
If no input file is named, or one is named as a single dash, the
standard input is read.
.sp
The default sorting key is the entire line.  Ordering is
lexicographic, by bytes, in machine collating sequence.
.sp
Sort can be used on arbitrarily large files.  It is limited only by
the amount of temporary file space it can consume.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
OPTIONS
.in +5
.br
.in +10
.ti -10
-b	Ignore leading blanks, spaces and tabs, in the key.

.ti -10
-d	Dictionary order: only letters, digits and blanks are
	significant in comparisons.

.ti -10
-f	Fold lower case onto upper case in comparisons.

.ti -10
-i	Ignore the non-printing characters in comparisons.

.ti -10
-m	Merge only.  The inputs are already sorted and are merged,
	not sorted again.

.ti -10
-n	Sort by integral number.  The key is taken as a signed
	decimal integer, a leading minus being part of the number,
	and what follows a decimal point is compared next.

.ti -10
-o file	Send the output to the named file.  "sort file -o file"
	sorts a file in place.

.ti -10
-r	Reverse.  Sort backwards.

.ti -10
-tc	The field separator is the character c rather than a run of
	blanks.

.ti -10
-T dir	Put the temporary files in dir rather than in /tmp.

.ti -10
-u	Unique: of lines that compare equal, only the first is kept.

.ti -10
-c	Check only.  Report the first line that is out of order,
	and with -u the first that is not unique.  One input file
	only.

.in -10
.sp
The options may be combined: "sort -b -d -f" is "sort -bdf".
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
FIELDS
.in +5
.br
A key may be confined to a part of the line.  A field is a run of
blanks and what follows it, or, with -t, whatever lies between two
separators.
.sp
.in +10
.ti -10
+pos	A key starts at field pos of the line, counting fields from
	zero.  "+0" is the whole line, which is also the default.

.ti -10
-pos	A key ends at field pos.  Without it the key runs to the
	end of the line.

.in -10
.sp
A key takes the flags that follow the plus and applies them to itself
alone, so "sort +1 -n" sorts by the second field as a number and
"sort +0.1" starts the key at the second character of the first field.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
EXAMPLES
.in +5
.br
To sort a file by its second column:
.sp
.in +5
.br
.bd
sort +1 file
.sp
.in -5
To sort a list of numbers:
.sp
.in +5
.br
.bd
sort -n file
.sp
.in -5
To sort a file in place:
.sp
.in +5
.br
.bd
sort file -o file
.sp
.in -5
To sort the password file by numerical user I.D., the fields being
separated by colons:
.sp
.in +5
.br
.bd
sort +2 -t: -n /etc/passwd
.sp
.in -5
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
NOTES
.in +5
.br
This is v7's sort.  The sort (1) page that shipped with 1.6 gives its
usage as -bdfnrtx and never says what the u or the x is, and it says
nothing at all about -c, -m or -T, which v7's source has and which are
kept here.  Its usage line and its flag scan disagree with each other
about the rest, and the scan was taken as the truth: -x is in neither
source nor this program.
.sp
Nothing is sorted in place through the -o name until the input has
been read, so -o may name one of the input files; when the input is
being merged rather than sorted, that file is copied aside first.
.sp
The temporary files are named stmnnnnnaa under the directory -T
names, or under /usr/tmp and then /tmp without it.  They are removed
when the sort ends, including when it is interrupted.
.sp
How much of the input is held at once is fixed by what is left of the
64k address space after the program, and the sort is carried out in
as many passes over temporary files as that makes necessary.  The
temporary directory is the only limit on the size of the input.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SEE ALSO
.in +5
.br
comm (1), unique (1)
